    "lawCode": "Add Art 29-A §520, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6339\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in\nrelation to creating a solar array pollinator benefit program\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo make it state policy to encourage the development on solar array\nsites of landscapes that are beneficial to pollinators.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Provides the short title for the act.\n\nSection 2: Amends the agriculture and markets law by adding a new\narticle, 29-A, which creates the pollinator support program.\n\nSection 3: States that the act shall take effect 90 days after this\nbill shall have become law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nAccording to the New York State Pollinator Taskforce, New York State\nis home to more than 450 pollinator species that are currently under\nthreat from habitat loss and fragmentation, colony collapse disorder,\nparasites, exposure to toxins, and other stressors. In 2016, it was\nestimated that New York State had lost fifty percent of its managed\npollinator colonies and seventy percent of its commercial migratory\nbees. Wild pollinators have also seen their numbers suffer, with the\nDepartment of Environmental Conservation listing seven species of bees\nand twenty-seven species of butterfly or moths as being Species of\nGreatest Conservation Need.\n\nThis is a critical issue since New York has 7.3 million acres in\nagriculture production, and many of our leading crops, such as apples,\nalfalfa, soybeans, cabbage, pumpkins, and berries, rely heavily on\npollination. Pollinator friendly habitats can take many forms, and be\na variety of sizes. Though many contiguous acres are best, studies\nhave shown that even \"pollinator enhancement strips\", small edge\npieces of land managed to promote the presence of pollinators, also\nhave a positive correlation with fruit growth.  Solar power facilities\noften provide ideal pollinator habitat because they constitute large\ncontiguous acreage, are maintained in sunny areas, and have low\nvegetation height. A variety of plant species with a high percentage\nof fortis (a herbaceous flowering plant like a sunflower), vegetation\nthat would allow nesting or overwintering such as shrubs or grasses,\nand at least three flowering species in each bloom period. Loss of\npollinators can impact the value of New York State's agricultural\nproduction, which currently totals $6.36 billion, and would seriously\ndiminish this pillar of the state's economy which provides 197,745\njobs.\n\nAt the same time, New York State is estimating the private industry\nwill build more than 6,000 acres of solar sites by 2020, and many of\nthese sites will be built on or near agricultural production sites.\nSome of these sites will choose to leave native plants around the\nsolar site, and provide crucial habitat and forage for pollinators.\n\nGiven the critical state of New York's pollinators, some solar site\nowners have made public claims that their land management practices\ndirectly benefit pollinators, and are therefore more eco-friendly, in\nan attempt to better market the solar energy they produce.  Given the\nwide support for pollinators currently, it is important to establish\nstandards for solar sites that wish to claim they are pollinator\nfriendly, so that consumers know that they owners of such sites are\ndelivering on their claims. This legislation would create a voluntary\nstandard for owners who wish to publically claim that their solar site\nis beneficial to pollinators, including minimum standards for\nbiodiversity and land management practices.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo be determined\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect within 90 days.\n",
