    "lawCode": "Amd §16, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6339A\n\nSPONSOR: RITCHIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in\nrelation to guidelines for pollinator protection\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo make it state policy to encourage the development on solar array\nsites of landscapes that are beneficial to pollinators by establishing\nminimum state guidelines.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 provides the Commissioner of Agriculture and Markets the\nauthority to develop minimum guidelines for vegetation management for\npersons or entities claiming that their property, including electric\ngenerating systems, is pollinator friendly. The section directs that the\nminimum guidelines must provide guidance on both short-term and long-\nterm property management practices, which provide and maintain native\nvegetation that protects pollinators. The section does not restrict any\nfarming practices that do not make such claims.\n\nSection 2 states that the act shall take effect 90 days after this bill\nshall have become law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAccording to the New York State Pollinator Taskforce, New York State is\nhome to more than 450 pollinator species that are currently under threat\nfrom habitat loss and fragmentation, colony collapse disorder, para-\nsites, exposure to toxins, and other stressors. In 2016, it was esti-\nmated that New York State had lost over fifty percent of its managed\npollinator colonies and over seventy percent of its commercial migratory\nbees. Wild pollinators have also seen their numbers suffer, with the\nDepartment of Environmental Conservation listing seven species of bees\nand twenty-seven species of butterfly or moths as being Species of\nGreatest Conservation Need.\n\nThis is a critical issue since New York has 7.3 million acres in agri-\nculture production, and many of our leading crops, such as apples,\nalfalfa, soybeans, cabbage, pumpkins, and berries, rely heavily on poll-\nination. Pollinator friendly habitats can take many forms, and be a\nvariety of sizes. Though many contiguous acres are best, studies have\nshown that even \"pollinator enhancement strips\", small edge pieces of\nland managed to promote the presence of pollinators, also have a posi-\ntive correlation with fruit growth.  Solar power facilities often\nprovide ideal pollinator habitat because they constitute large contig-\nuous acreage, are maintained in sunny areas, and have low vegetation\nheight. A variety of plant species with a high percentage of forbs (a\nherbaceous flowering plant like a sunflower), vegetation that would\nallow nesting or overwintering such as shrubs or grasses, and at least\nthree flowering species in each bloom period. Loss of pollinators can\nimpact the value of New York State's agricultural production, which\ncurrently totals $6.36 billion, and would seriously diminish this pillar\nof the state's economy which provides 197,745 jobs.\n\nAt the same time, New York State is estimating the private industry will\nbuild more than 6,000 acres of solar sites by 2020, and many of these\nsites will be built on or near agricultural production sites.  Some of\nthese sites will choose to leave native plants around the solar site,\nand provide crucial habitat and forage for pollinators.  Given the crit-\nical state of New York's pollinators, some solar site owners have made\npublic claims that their land management practices directly benefit\npollinators, and are therefore more eco-friendly, in an attempt to\nbetter market the solar energy they produce.\n\nGiven the wide support for pollinators currently, it is important to\nestablish standards for solar sites that wish to claim they are pollina-\ntor friendly, so that consumers know that the owners of such sites are\ndelivering on their claims. This legislation would direct the Commis-\nsioner of Agriculture and Markets to create guidelines for owners who\nwish to publically claim that their property, including solar sites, is\nbeneficial to pollinators. Such guidelines would include short-term and\nlong-term minimum standards for biodiversity and land management prac-\ntices. This legislation promotes greater pollinator protection, but does\nso without restricting farming practices for farms who do not make\npublic claims regarding pollinator benefits.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect within 90 days.\n",
